## Low-Dimensional Perovskites with Diammonium and Monoammonium Alternant Cations for High-Performance Photovoltaics
Low-dimensional Ruddlesden–Popper (LDRP) perovskites are a current theme in solar energy research as researchers attempt to fabricate stable photovoltaic devices from them. However, poor exciton dissociation and insufficiently fast charge transfer slows the charge extraction in these devices, resulting in inferior performance. 1,4-Butanediamine (BEA)-based low-dimensional perovskites are designed to improve the carrier extraction efficiency in such devices. Structural characterization using single-crystal X-ray diffraction reveals that these layered perovskites are formed by the alternating ordering of diammonium \((\mathsf{B E A}^{2+})\) and monoammonium \((\mathsf{M A}^{+})\) cations in the interlayer space (B-ACI) with the formula \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{n}\mathsf{P b}_{n}1_{n+1}\). Compared to the typical LDRP counterparts, these B-ACI perovskites deliver a wider light absorption window and lower exciton binding energies with a more stable layered perovskite structure. Additionally, ultrafast transient absorption indicates that B-ACI perovskites exhibit a narrow distribution of quantum well widths, leading to a barrier-free and balanced carrier transport pathway with enhanced carrier diffusion (electron and hole) length over \(350\;\mathsf{n m}\). A perovskite solar cell incorporating BEA ligands achieves record efficiencies of \(14.86\%\) for \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{13}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) and \(17.39\%\) for \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{C s}_{0.15}(\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{M A}_{0.17})_{2.85}\mathsf{P b}_{3}(\mathsf{I}_{0.83}\mathsf{B r}_{0.17})_{10}\) without hysteresis. Furthermore, the triple cations B-ACI devices can retain over \(90\%\) of their initial power conversion efficiency when stored under ambient atmospheric conditions for \(2400\,\mathsf{h}\) and show no significant degradation under constant illumination for over \(500\,\mathsf{h}\).
stability,[3,4] insensitivity to moisture and ultralow self-doping effect.[5–8] Unlike the more roundly expanded 3D perovskites, LDRP perovskites with chemical formula of \((\mathsf{A})_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{n-1}\mathsf{P b}_{n}\mathsf{I}_{3n+1}\) (where A is alkylammonium cation and \(n\) is the number of inorganic slabs) are considered as infinite nanoplatelets with atomic or molecular size obtained by quantizing the number of \([\mathsf{P b I}_{6}]^{4-}\) octahedra layers between organic ligands which causes quantum and dielectric confinement along one axis.[9–12] These ultrathin perovskite nanoplatelets are assembled together by intercalating organic cations (Van der Waals force), which protects the degradation of lattice by water and oxygen, resistance migration and maintains the structural integrity of the 2D and quasi-2D (q-2D) perovskites.[13–15] As a result, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) prepared by LDRP perovskite exhibit good stability.[16–20] Although hydrophobic slabs of LDRP perovskites prevent corrosion form water and oxygen, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the PSCs are much lower than that of 3D perovskite devices. As the high dielectric
Low-dimensional Ruddlesden–Popper (LDRP) perovskites have recently emerged as the viable photovoltaic materials due to their large formation energy,[1,2] superior photothermal
constant and exciton binding energy of LDRP perovskite not only narrow the optical absorption windows, but also confine electron–hole pairs to form tight bound excitons.[21–23]
Moreover, the LDRP perovskites are generally composed of multiple quantum wells (QWs), resulting in slow charge transfer between perovskite QWs that reduced carrier extraction efficiency.[24–27]
Recently, low-dimensional perovskites formed by alternately ordering of guanidinium and methylammonium (MA) cations in the interlayer space (ACI) has been a reasonable strategy to increasing PCEs of low-dimensional PSCs. Compared with the common LDRP perovskites, ACI perovskites show narrower bandgaps \((E_{\mathrm{g}})\) that enhanced optical absorption, leading to the PCE over \(14\%\) .[28] However, the improvement of PCE is at the expense of stability due to the reduced content of hydrophobic slabs. Another approach is to incorporate diammonium cations into 3D perovskite, developing a series of Dion–Jacobson (DJ) phase low-dimensional perovskite.[29,30] This special structure with reducing interlayer distance of inorganic slabs weaken the quantum confinement effect with enhanced PCE beyond \(13\%\) .[29–31] Meanwhile, the spacer of DJ perovskite with two amino groups at both ends can form hydrogen bonds with the inorganic slabs without any gaps, showing better stability compared to ACI and LDRP perovskite (van der Waals gap).[29,30] Nevertheless, the degree of dissociation of photogenerated carriers in DJ perovskite is much lower than that in ACI perovskite.[28,32] Furthermore, a spread of Egs in these low-dimensional perovskites due to varying degree of quantum confinement will lead to carrier localization and mobility reduction. Therefore, the conflict between stability and efficiency is a fundamental challenge for low-dimensional perovskite photovoltaic devices.
Here, we propose a strategy to improve light absorption capability and carrier extraction efficiency by designing 1,4-butanediamine (BEA) as the insulating organic cation to form a new type of low-dimensional perovskite with the formula of \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{n}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}\) , which contains diammonium \((\mathrm{BEA}^{2+})\) and monoammonium \((\mathrm{MA}^{+})\) alternating cations in the interlayer space ( \(B\) -ACI). Unlike the traditional ACI perovskites, \(B\) -ACI perovskites have hydrogen bond (between BEA and inorganic slabs), which result in more stable layered perovskite structure.[30] In addition, \(B\) -ACI perovskites adopted fewer long-chain organic ligands, which can significantly improve carrier transport performance compared with DJ perovskites.[28] Therefore, \(B\) -ACI perovskite has the advantages of both ACI and DJ perovskites. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction reveals the \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{n}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}\) have higher crystal symmetry and shorter interlayer distance that decrease the Egs compared with \((\mathrm{BA})_{2}\) \(\mathrm{MA}_{n-1}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}\) (BA: \(n\) -butylammonium, \(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) ) perovskite counterparts. Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) and ultraviolet–visible absorption also confirm that \(B\) -ACI perovskites have wide absorption window. Density functional theory (DFT) suggests that \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) is a direct bandgap semiconductor with lower exciton binding energy compared with \((\mathrm{BA})_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) . Meanwhile, \(B\) -ACI perovskite constructs a narrow distribution of quantum well widths, leading to efficient carrier transport pathway. As a result, the barriers of charge transport are efficiently reduced, making balanced carriers (electron and hole) diffusion length over \(350\ \mathrm{nm}\) with enhanced mobility. Sequentially, PSCs prepared with \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) exhibit an improved PCE of \(14.86\%\) . Furthermore, the mixed cations (FA, MA, and Cs) PSCs with
BEA as the interlayer spacer delivered a record PCE of \(17.39\%\) , which is the highest efficiency for ACI perovskite devices. In addition, the \(B\) -ACI perovskite devices exhibit higher stability under humidity, heating and light soaks than that of LDRP perovskite devices due to alternating hydrogen bonding interactions between \(\mathrm{BEA}^{2+}\) cations and inorganic slabs.
\((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) was investigated as the typical object of \(B\) -ACI \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{n}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}\) unless otherwise stated, since it had conspicuous features of low-dimensional perovskite. As shown in Figure 1a, we synthesized the typical flake \(B\) -ACI perovskite and selected the large single crystals (Figure S1, Supporting Information) to analyze the structure of the perovskite. The structure of \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) was solved and refined by using single crystal X-ray crystallography and the relevant data are presented in Table S1 (Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 1b, \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) belongs to the triclinic space group of P-1 with lattice parameters of \(a=8.7916(4)\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , \(b=8.8098(3)\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , and \(c=22.3335(10)\mathrm{~\AA~}\) . The alternating arrangement of BEA and MA, which act as spacers between the perovskite layers, enables \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) to have typical 2D \(B\) -ACI perovskite characteristics. These results are also suitable for low- \(_n\) values perovskite \(\lceil n=1\) and 2; Figure S2, Supporting Information). In Figure S3 (Supporting Information), the experimental X-ray diffraction (XRD) data of \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) power is in good agreement with the theoretical calculation determined by single-crystal XRD analysis, indicating successfully obtained \(B\) -ACI perovskite by simple combining \((\mathrm{BEA})\mathrm{I}_{2}\) , MAI, and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in a stoichiometric ratio (0.5:3:3). In addition, Density Functional Theory (Figure 1c) calculation manifests that \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) is a direct bandgap semiconductor (at Z point, \(E_{\mathrm{g}}=1.589\ \mathrm{eV},\) close to the experimental value in the following discussion).
We then studied the morphological evolution and crystal orientation of \(B\) -ACI perovskite films. The morphology of the \(B\) -ACI perovskite was investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) as shown in Figure 1d. It exhibits a full coverage and single crystal-like film with typical 2D perovskite features with large grains. Cross-sectional SEM image of the smooth \(B\) -ACI perovskite film is shown in Figure 1d (right, down) with the thickness about \(350\,\textrm{n m}\) that matching carrier diffusion length (in the following discussion). As shown in Figure 1e (X-ray diffraction), the \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film exhibits excellent crystallinity with three obvious diffraction peaks of (111), (202) and (313), which represent three dominant planes of \(B\) -ACI \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) perovskite. This script is also evident in low \(n\) value of \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{n}\mathrm{Pb}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{3n+1}\) \(\lceil n=1\) and 2; Figure S4, Supporting Information). To further probe the \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) orientation with respect to the substrate, 2D grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) measurement was carried out. As shown in Figure 1f, the \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film exhibits sharp, discrete Bragg spots along the same rings, indicating a textured polycrystalline film in which the crystal domains are oriented with their (101) planes parallel to the substrate surface (Figure S5, Supporting Information). This crystal orientation is favor of efficient separation of carriers.[5]
We further explored the optical bandgap and excitonic features of \(B\) -ACI perovskite. Ultraviolet–visible absorption and photoluminescence (PL) of the low-dimensional perovskite films are carried out and the spectra are presented in
Figure 2a,b. The absorption and PL of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) film yield band-edge energies of \(1.651~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\approx\!751\ \mathrm{nm})\) and \(1.667~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\approx\!744\,\mathrm{\nm})\) , respectively, having an obviously redshifted compared with that of \((\mathsf{B A})_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{2}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) (1.87 and \(1.89~\mathrm{eV}\) ). Similar scenarios are also available for low- \(n\) values perovskite (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the optical absorption coefficient spectra of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{n}\mathsf{P b}_{n}\mathsf{I}_{3n+1}\) are shown in Figure S7 (Supporting Information). The results clearly show that the visible light absorption coefficient of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) is as large as that of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\).[33] The large visible light absorption is critical for achieving high PCE cells based on this material. As shown in Figure 2c, the redshift in absorption observed for the \( B \)-ACI perovskites, relative to the LDRP perovskites, holds firmly in the PL spectra as well. This result confirmed that dielectric properties have a significant effect on carrier luminescence and transport, which influence on carrier dissociation.[11] In addition, we used first-order perturbation theory and an infinite barrier approximation[34] to calculate the exciton binding energy of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) and \((\mathsf{B A})_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{2}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) (Note S1 and Figure S8, Supporting Information). The exciton binding energy of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) \((\approx\!120\ \mathrm{meV})\) is much smaller than that of \((\mathsf{B A})_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{2}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) \((\approx\!162\ \mathrm{meV})\) , leading to benefit to dissociate photogenerated carriers. Moreover, the average surface potential (SP; Figure 2d) of \( B \)-ACI perovskite \((\approx\!56\,\mathrm{\mV})\) is larger than that of LDRP perovskite \((\approx\!36\,\mathrm{\mV})\) , which suggests a larger downward surface band bending for the \( B \)-ACI perovskite film. The larger downward surface band bending
of active layer is in favor of charge-carrier separation and will result in higher fill factor, current density, and reduced hysteresis in the solar cells.[24] We further conducted the projected DOS of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) (Figure S9, Supporting Information), the VBM and CBM are all predominantly contributed by 3p orbitals, indicting good conductivity of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) perovskite.[35] These results confirmed that \( B \)-ACI perovskite had an excellent ability of exciton dissociation. Specifically, the Fermi levels and valence bands (VBs) of the \( B \)-ACI perovskites were measured by UPS (Figure 2e; Figure S10, Supporting Information) and the optical Eqs. derived from the Tauc plots were \(1.60~\mathrm{eV}\) \((n=3)\), \(1.80~\mathrm{eV}\) \((n=2)\), and \(2.03~\mathrm{eV}\) \((n=1)\) (Figure S11, Supporting Information). The detailed energy levels were presented in Figure 2f. The high symmetry (Table S2, Supporting Information), short interlayer distance \((2.878\,\mathrm{\AA})\); Figure S12, Supporting Information, LDRP perovskite was \(7\,\mathrm{\AA}\)),[8] weak dielectric properties of the \( B \)-ACI perovskites are the main reasons for the narrow Eqs (Note S2, Supporting Information). Note that the hydrogen bonds between \(\mathsf{B E A}^{2+}\) and \([\mathsf{P b}]_{6}]^{4-}\) of \( B \)-ACI perovskite are stronger than Van der Waals force of LDRP perovskite, which also contributes to increasing light absorption and structural stability (Figure S13, Supporting Information).[30]
Femtosecond transient absorption (TA) were carried out to explore the precise QWs distribution of low-dimensional perovskite based on BEA and BA cations, since exciton resonance was more readily discernible at an early stage without being annihilated by continuum transitions.[13] Figure 3a shows
the TA spectrum of the control film \(\left(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\right)\) at various delay times. Obviously, the control film features distinct bleach peaks at 570, 604, and \(640\;\mathrm{nm}\), which can be assigned to perovskite phase with \(n=2\), 3, and 4, respectively. Specifically, there appears a simultaneous rise in the signals for \(n=5+\) and \(n=\infty\), indicating bulk perovskite phases with large \(n\) values also exist in control film. This result confirmed that the control film comprises hybrid perovskite phases with diverse distribution of QWs.[13,25,26] In \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) films (Figure 3d), there are similar negative bleach signals at early delay times for excitonic resonances of \(n=2{-}4\), with only the BEA film having a slightly redshifted exciton absorption peak, indicating small bandgap of \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) as above mentioned. In addition, the fitted of main exciton bleach between 680 and \(750\;\mathrm{nm}\) confirmed that there is no isolated exciton absorption peak for \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film, which is indicative of narrow QWs among \(n\geq5\) phases (Figure S14, Supporting Information). This scenario predicates that \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) perovskite creates a flattened energy landscape and weakened quantum confinement mechanism that contributes to the carrier transmission. To understand different quantum confinement of \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) and \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) effect on carrier transport, TA kinetics were analyzed as shown in Figure 3b,e. In \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film, it can be found that the prominent photobleaching (PB) at \(n\leq4\) exciton resonance appears instantaneously, which demonstrates that the excitons are directly generated in the \(n=2\) \((570\;\mathrm{nm})\), \(n=3\) \((604\;\mathrm{nm})\), and \(n=4\) \((640\;\mathrm{nm})\) QWs. Following the fast buildup, substantial portion of the photogenerated excitons from \(n=2\), \(n=3\), and \(n=4\) phases will localize to \(n=\infty\) phases within \(0.4\;\mathrm{ps}\). This ultrafast decay time is well matched with the rapid PB establish time of the large- \(n\) (where \(n=\infty\)) phases \((720\;\mathrm{nm})\). However, in \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film, substantial portion of the photogenerated excitons from \(n=2\) and \(n=3\) phases will localize to \(n=4\) phases and \(n=\infty\) phases within \(0.3\;\mathrm{ps}\). Therefore, more efficient carrier transport pathway is established in \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film than the \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) one. This efficient carrier transport pathway can inhibit the formation of recombination centers to improve the carrier performance. Different carrier transport mechanisms are shown in Figure 3c,f.
To confirm whether the weakened quantum confinement mechanism in favor of carrier transport, time-resolved PL (TRPL) were used to study the carrier properties of lifetimes \((\tau)\), diffusion lengths \((L_{\mathrm{D}})\), and mobility \((M)\), respectively. TRPL measurements were conducted with bare \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) and \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) films and heterojunctions with [6,6]-phenyl- \(\cdot\mathrm{C}61\) -butyric acid methylester (PCBM) electron and \(^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}\) -tetrakis \((N,N\)-di- \(p\) -methoxyphenylamino)- \(9,9^{\prime}\) -spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) hole extraction materials, which were analyzed by charge-carrier extraction model. The effective PL lifetime is introduced as the \(\tau\) for calculating \(L_{\mathrm{D}}\) and \(M\).[36] As shown in Figure S15a (Supporting Information), the carrier lifetime of \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) \((121.6\;\mathrm{ns})\) are much larger than that of \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) \((45.2\;\mathrm{ns})\). With the relation of \(\tau_{\mathrm{FT}}=\left(\tau_{\mathrm{Heterojunction}}\cdot\tau_{\mathrm{Perovskite}}\right)/\left(\tau_{\mathrm{Perovskite}}-\tau_{\mathrm{Heterojunction}}\right)\), the electron extraction times \((\tau_{\mathrm{FT}})\) are estimated to be 2.9 and 3.3 ns for the \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}/\mathrm{PCBM}\) and \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}/\mathrm{PCBM}\) heterojunctions (Figure S15b, Supporting Information), respectively. Then the corresponding electron extraction efficiencies
are calculated to be \(98\%\) and \(93\%\). Similarly, the hole extraction times \((\tau_{\mathrm{HT}})\) are estimated to be 6.4 and 27.8 ns for the \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}/\mathrm{Spiro-OMeTAD}\) and \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}/\mathrm{Spiro-OMeTAD}\) heterojunctions, respectively. Then, the corresponding hole extraction efficiencies are calculated to be \(95\%\) and \(62\%\). Detailed calculations are presented in Note S3 and Table S3 (Supporting Information). Then 1D PL carrier diffusion model[36] was used to determine \(L_{\mathrm{D}}\) (see Note S4 in the Supporting Information for details). The \(M\) is calculated based on the equation of \(L_{\mathrm{D}}=\sqrt{D\cdot\tau}\) and \(D=M k T/q\). The \(M\) of \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) was calculated to \(8.6\times10^{-1}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{S}^{-1}\), while the \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) was calculated to \(9.1\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{S}^{-1}\). It is conspicuous that the carrier of \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film has longer lifetime, longer \(L_{\mathrm{D}}\), and larger \(M\) compared with that of \(\left(\mathrm{BA}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{2}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) film. The extracted parameters are summarized in Table 1. Notably, it reported that the electron transferred from lower- \(n\) to larger- \(n\) phases, whereas the hole transferred from larger- \(n\) to lower- \(n\) phases in QWs.[8] The lower- \(n\) \((n\leq3)\) perovskite phases enable significant barriers that against to hole transport. Interestingly, the electron diffusion of \(\left(\mathrm{BEA}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{MA}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{I}_{10}\) is \(486\pm9\;\mathrm{nm}\), which is equivalent to the hole diffusion \((391\pm6\;\mathrm{nm})\), indicating that the balanced carrier transport mechanism is realized in \(B\)-ACI perovskite.
To rationalize the remarkable performance of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) devices, \(C{-}V\) measurement by using the Mott–Schottky equation[37] (Note S5, Supporting Information) was executed to determine the charge density profile of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) and \((\mathsf{B A})_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{2}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) (Figure S21, Supporting Information). Clearly, both of two devices are fully depleted and the trap state densities of the devices are small, so the trapping of free charge will have a great effect on the \(J{-}V\) characteristics of the devices. Nevertheless, a higher carrier density \((\approx\!3.3\times10^{16})\) was obtained for \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) compared with \((\mathsf{B A})_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{2}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) \((\approx\!4.6\times10^{15})\) one, which indicates that the \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\) is more advantageous to extract the charges, leading to more efficient devices. The mixed cation (FA, MA, and Cs) PSCs have been widely recognized with improved device performance. We further
introduced BEA cation into mixed cation 3D perovskite to prepare active layers. Previous reports have demonstrated that mixed cations 3D perovskites can be sliced into quasi-2D perovskites using organic ligands.[38,39] As shown in Figure S22 (Supporting Information), the absorption spectra of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{C s}_{0.15}(\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{M A}_{0.17})_{2.85}\mathsf{P b}_{3}(\mathsf{I}_{0.83}\mathsf{B r}_{0.17})_{10}\) film is dominated by excitonic peaks, indicating BEA based mixed cation perovskite is also a series of quasi-2D/3D mixture. Furthermore, XRD patterns and SEM images (Figure S23, Supporting Information) of BEA + mixed cations perovskites films demonstrate that the triple-A-site cation with BEA ligand \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{C s}_{0.15}(\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{M A}_{0.17})_{2.85}\mathsf{P b}_{3}(\mathsf{I}_{0.83}\mathsf{B r}_{0.17})_{10}\) perovskite is B-ACI structure, which derives from the structure of \((\mathsf{B E A})_{0.5}\mathsf{M A}_{3}\mathsf{P b}_{3}\mathsf{I}_{10}\).[40] Figure 4c shows the \(J{-}V\) characteristics of the mixed cation devices. The mixed cations
B-ACI perovskite solar cell shows the highest PCE of \(17.39\%\) with a current density \((J_{\mathrm{SC}})\) of \(21.89\ \mathrm{mA\ cm^{-2}}\), an open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{OC}})\) of \(1.10\ \mathrm{V}\) and a fill factor (FF) of 0.722. To the best of our knowledge, this is one of the best PCEs among the representative mixed cations low-dimensional perovskite solar cells (Table S5, Supporting Information). While BA based device exhibits the champion PCE of \(13.96\%\) with a \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(20.81\ \mathrm{mA\ cm^{-2}}\), a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.16\ \mathrm{V}\) and an FF of 0.613 (Figure 4b, inset). Furthermore, the mixed cations B-ACI perovskite-based device exhibits negligible hysteresis with PCE of \(17.39\%\) under reverse scan and \(17.31\%\) under forward scan (Figure S24, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the results (Figure S25, Supporting Information) show that the BEA-based triple-cation cells demonstrated a higher average PCE \((15.43\pm1.08\%)\) with narrower distribution than the BA-based triple-cation cells \((9.62\pm3.61\%)\). The improvement of PCE is mainly attributed to the high \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) and FF of B-ACI perovskite, which responsible for the wide light absorption window, long carriers \(L_{\mathrm{D}}\), and high carrier mobility.
Considering the actual conditions in the operation of solar cells, we conducted stability tests under atmospheric humidity, continuous illumination and high temperature using \((\mathrm{BEA})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.15}(\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17})_{2.85}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}(\mathrm{I}_{0.83}\mathrm{Br}_{0.17})_{10}\) and \((\mathrm{BA})_{2}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.1}(\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{MA}_{0.17})_{1.5}\mathrm{Pb}_{3}(\mathrm{I}_{0.83}\mathrm{Br}_{0.17})_{10}\) perovskite solar cells, respectively. Figure 4d shows the evolution of normalized PCE over time for encapsulated devices stored in ambient atmospheric under dark (humidity \(\approx35\%\)). B-ACI devices exhibit good environmental stability, retaining \(90\%\) of their initial PCEs after aging \(2400\ \mathrm{h}\). However, LDRP only retain \(85\%\) their initial PCEs. Figure 4e illustrates the continuous illumination stability of the devices. The B-ACI devices maintain \(93\%\) of their initial efficiency under continuous illumination after \(500\ \mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. However, the LDRP devices only maintain \(82\%\) of their initial PCEs. Thermal stability of the devices is also executed under \(85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) without encapsulation as shown in Figure 4f. The B-ACI devices lost \(80\%\) of their initial efficiency after \(100\ \mathrm{h}\). Contrastively, LDRP devices completely lost their function after \(90\ \mathrm{h}\). In addition, the stability against moisture is also performed via placing the devices in a humidity chamber (relative humidity of \(85\%\)) with simple encapsulation. As shown in Figure 4g, the B-ACI devices can retain over \(80\%\) of their initial PCEs after \(300\ \mathrm{h}\), while LDRP devices retain only \(70\%\) of their initial PCEs. Detailed PCE attenuation values are also shown in Figure S26 (Supporting Information). The outstanding stability performance of B-ACI devices are mainly attributed to the inhibition of the formation large \(n\)-value perovskite \((n\geq5)\), high symmetry and strong interaction between interlayers, which effectively reduces ion migration and prevent decomposition under water, oxygen, and light.[11]
In conclusion, we have demonstrated a new type low-dimensional perovskite (B-ACI) with BEA organic ligand for wide light absorption windows and efficient carrier extraction efficiency. The B-ACI perovskite delivered an efficient carrier localization process, leading to a barrier-free and balanced carrier transport pathway, and enabling balanced carrier diffusion length over \(350\ \mathrm{nm}\) as well as enhanced mobility. Employed B-ACI perovskite as the active layers, we achieved champion device efficiency of \(17.39\%\), which is much higher than that of LDRP perovskite \((13.96\%)\). Moreover, B-ACI perovskite has hydrogen
bond between BEA and inorganic slabs, thus the devices show much more stable against humidity, heating and light soaks. The results demonstrate the new B-ACI perovskite is a promising material to overcoming the performance-stability compromise in low-dimensional perovskite devices.
